BCC_Q({"y": 2017, "n": 13, "part": "C", "topic": "logic", "title": "Beehive", "answer": 2, "story": "<p>A bear studies how many hexagons in a honeycomb contain honey. For\neach hexagon, the bear records how many <span><em>other<\/em><\/span>\nhexagons touching this hexagon contain honey. So this number could be 0,\n1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or 6. The results of the bear’s study are below.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"A description of the diagram follows.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:50.0%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<button onclick=\"hideSeek(1658158446)\" style=\"box-shadow:none\">Hide/Reveal Description of Diagram for Beehive<\/button>\n<div id=\"1658158446\" style=\"display: none;\">\n<p>Sixteen hexagons are arranged, with no gaps and no overlaps, into a shape like a diamond. There are seven columns of hexagons in the shape. The number of hexagons in the seven columns are one, two, three, four, three, two, one.<\/p>\n<p>The hexagon in the first column touches the two hexagons in the second column. Each hexagon in the second column touches two hexagons in the third column. Each hexagon in the third column touches two hexagons in the fourth column. This pattern is repeated on the other side with the hexagon in the seventh column touching the two hexagons in the sixth column, each hexagon in the sixth column touching two hexagons in the fifth column, and so on.<\/p>\n \nEach hexagon contains a number. The number in each hexagon, going from top to bottom in each column, is as follows:\n<ul>\n<li>First column: 1<\/li>\n<li>Second column: 3, 2<\/li>\n<li>Third column: 2, 3, 4<\/li>\n<li>Fourth column: 2, 4, 4, 1<\/li>\n<li>Fifth column: 2, 3, 3<\/li>\n<li>Sixth column: 3, 2<\/li>\n<li>Seventh column: 0<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<hr class=\"longdesc\"/>\n<\/div>", "question": "<p>How many hexagons contain honey?<\/p>\n<ol class=\"mc\">\n<li>7<\/li>\n<li>8<\/li>\n<li>9<\/li>\n<li>10<\/li>\n<\/ol>", "answerHtml": "<p>(C) 9<\/p>", "explanation": "<p>One way to solve this task is to start from the cells for which the\ncontent of their adjacent cells is known. It is the case for two cells,\nthat have a circle in the picture below:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>All the adjacent cells of the cells with a 0 are empty<\/li>\n<li>All the adjacent cells of the cells with a 4 are full<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>We will use yellow to represent honey in a hex, grey to represent no\nhoney, and brown for undetermined.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"The hexagon in the seventh row has a 0. The two hexagons in the sixth column touch this hexagon and are grey. The bottom hexagon in the third column has a 4. The four hexagons touching this hexagon are yellow.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:50.0%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>After that, we can easily find some others cells, near the zone that\nwe have changed, for which the content of their adjacent cells is now\nknown. For instance the cell with a circle in the picture below, has\nonly 1 full adjacent cell. It is the one already coloured in yellow, just\nabove it. So its other adjacent cell is empty.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"The bottom hexagon in the fourth row has a 1. The hexagon above it is yellow. The two hexagons beside it are changed to grey.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:50.0%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>Then, the cell with a 3 on it, which we just coloured grey, must have\nexactly 3 full adjacent cells. We have already two of them on its left,\nthe one on the right is empty, so the one above it must be full.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"The bottom hexagon in the fifth row has a 3 and is coloured grey. The hexagon above it is changed to yellow.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:50.0%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>We can go on from one adjacent cell to the others, in order to cover\nall the beehive.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"A description of the diagram follows\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:50.0%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<button onclick=\"hideSeek(1658158450)\" style=\"box-shadow:none\">Hide/Reveal Description of Diagram for Beehive Explanation<\/button>\n<div id=\"1658158450\" style=\"display: none;\">\n<p>All sixteen hexagons are coloured either grey or yellow. The numbers in the hexagons in each column, along with their corresponding colourings are as follows.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>First column: 1 (yellow)<\/li>\n<li>Second column: 3 (grey), 2 (yellow)<\/li>\n<li>Third column: 2 (grey), 3 (yellow), 4 (grey)<\/li>\n<li>Fourth column: 2 (grey), 4 (yellow), 4 (yellow), 1 (yellow)<\/li>\n<li>Fifth column: 2 (yellow), 3 (yellow), 3 (grey)<\/li>\n<li>Sixth column: 3 (grey), 2 (grey)<\/li>\n<li>Seventh column: 0 (yellow)<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<hr class=\"longdesc\"/>\n<\/div>", "connections": "<p>This task concerns <span><em>logic<\/em><\/span> and\n<span><em>inference<\/em><\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The logic in this problem is to understand that, for example, a cell\nmarked as 0 means that none of its neighbours contain honey. From this\nfact, we can <span><em>infer<\/em><\/span> further facts, and build up a\nsolution to the larger problem.<\/p>\n<p>The method of building a solution using inference is what is called a\n<span><em>bottom-up<\/em><\/span> algorithm: we start with a solution to a\nvery small part of problem (the “bottom” of the problem) and then build\nup larger and larger solutions until we have solved the entire problem\n(the “top” of the problem).<\/p>", "mc": 1});